The genealogy of this family, Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was a father of Isaac. And Isaac was a father of Jacob. And Jacob was a father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah the father of Peres and Zerah; and Tamar their mother. Peres was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Aram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salomo.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. And David was the father of Solomon. And Solomon's mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. And Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After this, the genealogy of the family continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Azor was the father of Sadok, Sadok was the father of Achim, and Achim was the father of Eleoud.
Eliud was the father of Elazar. Elazar was the father of Matan. Matan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. And Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
Thus there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 from David until the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon. And there were 14 from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary, and Joseph was engaged to her. But before they came together, it was discovered that she was with child. He had made her pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Youssef was a religious man. He didn't want to expose her publicly, so he decided to divorce her in the best possible way.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said to him, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary your wife into your home. For that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She is going to give birth to a boy. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and she will name him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God is with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord had commanded him. He took Mary into his house as wife.
But he did not love her even after she gave birth to a son. And Joseph named him Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This took place while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed. And all the people in Jerusalem were also disturbed.
Herod called the priests, the chiefs of the people, and the teachers of the law, and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they replied. "For this is what the prophet has written. 'The voice of one crying in the wilderness: Make ready the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.'"
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origin is from old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He found out from them the exact time the star appeared.
He sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go! Search diligently for the child. However you find him, bring me word. In this way, I too may come to know and go worship him as well."
After the Magi heard the reply of the king, they departed on their way. The star which they had seen when they were in the east preceded them. Finally it came and stood directly above the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. Thus they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. After that, they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another way. The family of Jesus flees to Egypt.
When the Magi had departed, Joseph saw a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Arise!" said the angel. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod will seek the child to destroy him."
Joseph's family. At night, he went to Egypt with the child, his mother Mary, and Joseph's pregnant wife, who was about to give birth.
Stay like this until King Herod dies. In this way, the words spoken by the Lord through the prophet were fulfilled, where he said, "I have decided to call my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued his orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding areas. All the young boys who were two years old and under were to be killed. This corresponded with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
Thus were fulfilled the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he had spoken. He had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of weeping and deep lamentation. Rachel weeps for her children, refusing to be comforted, for they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) Jesus' family returns to Nazareth.
After Herod died, Joseph had a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child have died."
Thus Joseph resisted. Take the child and his mother Mary and return to the land of Israel.
But then he heard that Archelaus was the king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling instead of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After he received a warning in a dream, Joseph went back to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. Thus, what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. They had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and was preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Separate yourselves from your sins! The kingdom of the heavens is near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice is calling out, 'In the wilderness prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He wore a belt of skin around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people of Jerusalem and all of Judea and the entire district that surrounds the region across the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bring forth fruit worthy of repentance.
Do not suppose that you can enter the kingdom of heaven by saying, "We are the children of Abraham." I assure you that God is able to raise up children for Abraham from these stones.
The axe is placed in the roots of the tree. Each tree must not bring any benefit, it will be cut down. It will be placed in the fire.
"I baptize you with water and call you to repent of your sins. But after me will come one mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire."
From his hand, with him is the saw, and he has put it down, removing the straw from on top of the barley. He gathers his wheat into the granary. As for the husks, he burns them with unquenchable fire." (Baptism of Jesus)
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. Jesus said to him, "You yourself must be baptized by me. Why have you come to me?"
Jesus answered: "Let her alone for now. It is our right to do so. This fulfills God's holy plan." And then Moses John died.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up out of the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love. I am very proud of him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, Satan tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
Then the devil took him to the holy city and placed him on the pinnacle of the temple. "If you are the Son of God," he said, "then command this stone to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city, had him stand at the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their splendor.
"If you bow down and worship me," he said, "I will give you all this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus begins to preach.
John was put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum, which is beside the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
This is how the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled, who said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jewish people live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who are living now walk in a great darkness. They live now in a very dark land. But a great light will shine upon them.
From that time, Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent of your sins!" "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Meanwhile, they emptied their pockets and followed him.
After that, he saw other brothers, namely James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. When they were mending their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat, their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals sick people.
Jesus walked throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of the kingdom of God. He healed all the diseases and afflictions that the people suffered from.
His fame spread throughout Syria, and people brought to him all who were sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pains, others were possessed by demons, some were convulsing violently, and others were completely paralyzed. Jesus healed them all.
A large crowd followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region of the ten cities, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region located on the other side of the Jordan.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on a mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are those who have a spiritual need. The heavenly kingdom inspires.
Blessed are those who are sad, may God relieve their burden.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers. They will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for the sake of what must be done. The Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
Blessed are you when people revile you and speak all manner of evil against me. You are also blessed when they fabricate all kinds of malicious lies against me.
Be joyful and happy, your reward in heaven is great. In the same way, people hurt the prophets who lived a long time ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but is thrown out and trampled under people's feet.
You are the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden.
Furthermore, people don't light a bulb and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it in its base. This way, it lights up the whole house for everyone.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. So that they may see the good that you do. And give glory to your Father who is in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning to the law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish but to fulfill.
What I am about to tell you is a fact. Heaven and earth will disappear before even the smallest letter from the law disappears. Even the smallest stroke of a pen will not disappear from the law until everything is completed.
Do not neglect even the least of these commandments. And do not neglect to teach people to observe them. If you do this, you will be regarded as the least in the kingdom of heaven. Instead, go and do what these commandments teach and instruct them. In this way, you will be regarded as important in the kingdom of heaven."
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. To murder...
I have heard what was said to the people who lived a long time ago. It was said to them: 'You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever murders will be judged for that.'
But here's what we say to you. Don't be angry with your brother. Whoever is angry with his brother will be judged. Again, whoever says to his brother, "You fool!" must appear before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, "You donkey!" risks eternal fire in hell.
Imagine you are presenting your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother. Then come and offer your gift.
Imagine if someone had a claim against you and was taking you to court. Try to settle it quickly. Do that while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he might hand you over to the judge. The judge might hand you over to the officer. And you might be thrown into jail.
What I intend to tell you is true. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But this is what we are telling you. Even so that you don't look at the woman in the wrong way. Anyone who does this must have committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and throw it away. For it is profitable for you that one of your members would perish, rather than your whole body going into hell.
"If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. For your hand is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell. Divorce."
It was said, "If a man divorces his wife, he shall give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man is not to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful. Oaths
Again, I heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told: "Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord your God. You shall not break your solemn covenants made to Him."
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make promises like that at all. Don't make them in heaven's name. This is God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth. This is the footprint of God. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem. This is the city of the great King.
And do not make your oaths a means of hypocrisy in your hearts, lest your foot slip after it had been firm
Just let your "yes" mean "yes." Let your "no" mean "no." Anything more than this is from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, 'An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.'
But here's what we tell you, don't mess with a wicked person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, present him your left cheek."
If someone sues you to take your shirt, let them take your clothes too.
If someone detains you and asks you to walk a mile, walk two miles with them.
Give to anyone who asks of you. Do not turn your back on anyone who asks to borrow from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'You shall love your neighbor and hate your enemy.'
But here's what we tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who spite you.
Then you will be the sons of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends down rain from heaven and does not make it descend on one deed and another.
If you love those who love you, what reward do you expect? Even the tax collectors do that.
If you're just greeting your family, what more are you doing than others? Even people without religion do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing 'good deeds' in front of others. Do not do them so that people can see you. If you do that, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
"When you give to people who are in need, do not announce it by blowing trumpets. Do not be like those who only pretend to be holy. They announce what they do in the temples and in the streets. They want others to honor them. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward."
When you give to the poor, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving should be in secret, your father will reward you, he sees what you do in secret. Prayer.
When you pray, don't be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the synagogues and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I am about to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
"When you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who cannot be seen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret."
When you pray, do not babble repeatedly as the Gentiles do, for they suppose that they will be heard for their many words.
Don't worry, your father knows what you want even before you ask him.
Thus you should pray: "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Blessed be Your Kingdom, and Your Will is sanctified, just as in heaven, so also on earth.
Give us today our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Protect us from falling into sin when tempted. Deliver us from evil.'
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do this, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Prayer.
When you walk without eating, you are not as gloomy as those who resemble the righteous. They sadden their faces a lot. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am about to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you go away without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then others won't know that you are fasting, except your father who doesn't seem to be the one who knows. He will bring you your right. Your father sees what is done in secret. Keep your wealth in Paradise.
Do not store up treasures for yourselves on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in and steal.
Your heart is where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes no longer see. Your whole body becomes filled with darkness. If the light within you is darkness, then the darkness is truly great!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be loyal to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Don't stop.
Let me tell you, don't be afraid. Don't be afraid for your life, for what you eat or drink. And don't be afraid for your body, for what you wear. Isn't life greater than food? Aren't there more important things for the body than clothes?
Consider the birds of the air: they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?
Can worrying add one hour to your life?
And why worry about the outfit? Look at how wildflowers sprout. They don't work or manufacture clothing.
But that is what I am telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If this is how God clothes the wild grass, won't He clothe you even better? After all, grass is here only for today. Tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!
So don't worry. Don't say "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People without religion run after all these things. Your father in heaven knows you need them.
But put God's kingdom first. Do what God wants you to do. After that, all those things will be given to you as well.
Do not be concerned about tomorrow, for tomorrow will be concerned with itself. Each day has its own troubles.
Be fair when you judge other people.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You judge people so that they may judge you, and you measure people so that they may measure you.
You see a splinter of wood in your friend's eye, but you forget the beam in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, "Let me get some of that sawdust out of your eyes"? How can you say that expression and you have a piece of wood in your eye?
"O claimant! First, take the piece of wood out of your own eye. After that, you will be able to see clearly to take the splinter of wood out of your friend's eye."
"Cast not your pearls before swine. Give not that which is holy unto the dogs. If you do this, perhaps they will trample them underfoot. And then perhaps they will turn and rend you. Ask, seek, knock."
Ask, and it will be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Whoever seeks will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
They imposed your son to ask for bread. Which one of you will give him a stone?
Or suppose he asks for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
Even if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
"في كل شيء، اعمل للآخرين ما تريد منهم أن يفعلوه لك. هذا هو ما هو مكتوب في الشريعة وفي الأنبياء. الأبواب الكبيرة والصغيرة"
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate, for the gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to destruction and hell, and many are those who enter through it.
But the gate is narrow and the way that leads to life is narrow, and few are the people who find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
Do you know what they are really like, from what they do? "Do people pick grapes from the thorny tree? Do they pick figs from the vine?"
Also, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree, if it bears good fruit; and a bad tree, if it bears bad fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
A tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven; but only those who do the will of my Father who is in heaven will enter.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then we clearly tell them: "I have never known you. Get away from me, you evil people!" The wise builders and the foolish ones.
So, everyone who hears my words and puts them into practice is like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
Rain falls. Water rises. Winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not act on them will be like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
The rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud bang.
Jesus continued to say all these things. The people were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the mountain. A large crowd followed him.
A man who had a skin disease came and knelt down in front of Jesus. He said, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me 'clean.'"
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "'Be clean!'" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman centurion believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him and asked for his help.
"Sir," he said, "my servant is lying in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered and said, "My lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but only speak a word, and my servant shall be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes. I say to that one, 'Come,' and he comes. I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to the disciples who were following him, "Truly I say to you, I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel."
Let me tell you, many from the East and the West will come, take their places at the feast in the Kingdom of the Heavens, sit down with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But those who thought of themselves as being of the Kingdom, they are now cast out, into the darkness. There they will weep and gnash their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! It will be as you believed." And at that very hour his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying in bed, and she was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her with his hand, and the fever left her. She got up and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus all the people who had demons. He cast out the demons with a word. He healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said: "He was wounded for our transgressions, he was crushed for our iniquities; upon him was the punishment that made us whole, and by his wounds we are healed." (Isaiah 53:4) Following Jesus requires sacrifice.
Jesus saw the crowd around him, so he told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the scribes came to him and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "Foxes have dens and birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Master, let me go and bury my father first of all."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm
Jesus embarked on a ship, and his disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a terrible storm came upon the lake. The waves beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
His disciples went and woke him up, saying, "Master! Save us! We are going to perish!"
Jesus replied to them, "It is because of your little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they became perfectly calm.
The disciples were astonished. They asked, "Who is this man? Even the wind and the waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men who are demon-possessed.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. He encountered two men who were possessed by demons. They had come from the tombs. The two men were so violent that no one could travel on that road.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they screamed. "Did you come here to punish us before the appointed time for judgment against us?"
It was not far, there was a large herd of pigs eating.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us to the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. Then the entire herd of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the lake, and they drowned in the water.
Those who were serving the pigs ran away. They went to the country and told about everything. They told the people what had happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
Then all the city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to depart from their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus got into a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and came to his own country.
Some men brought him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Jesus saw that they had faith. He said to the man, "Do not think about your hopelessness, my son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the Pharisees said to themselves, "This man speaks blasphemies!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Stand up and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," he said. "Take your mat and go home."
The man sat down and went home.
When the crowd saw this, they were amazed. They praised God, who had given such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
As Jesus was going from there, he saw a man named Matthew. He was sitting at the tax office. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Matthew rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "Those who are well do not need a doctor, but those who are sick do."
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice' (Hosea 6:6). I did not come to call the righteous, who think they are right with God, to follow me. I came to call sinners to follow me." Jesus was asked about fasting.
One day John's disciples came to Jesus and said, "We and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. The new piece will pull away from the old one. This will increase the tearing.
The people do not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst. The wine will spill out, and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone who pours new wine into old wineskins is a fool. Both the wine and the wineskins will be destroyed. Instead, everyone who has new wine should pour it into new wineskins. In this way, both the wine and the wineskins will be preserved." A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a leader of the synagogue came. He fell on his knees before Jesus and said, "My daughter has just died, but come and put your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus rose and walked with him. And his disciples did as he did.
At that moment, a woman came up behind Jesus. She was sick and bleeding. This condition had been with her for 12 years. She touched the edge of his robe.
I thought, "Only to touch his clothes, and I shall be cured."
Jesus turned and saw her. "Woman," he said to her, "do not despair, your faith has saved you." The woman was healed at that very moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go, the girl is not dead, she is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the audience was sent out, Issa entered. He took the girl by her hand, and she stood up.
The news about what Jesus had done spread throughout the whole region. Jesus heals two blind men.
When Jesus went out from there, two blind men followed Him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus entered the house, the blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" And they answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "What you believed in will befall you."
Now they could see again. Jesus warned them sternly, "Make sure no one finds out about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it in all that region.
As they were going out, they brought another demon-possessed man to Jesus. The demons in him had control over him and he could not speak.
When the evil spirit had gone out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "What is this? A new teaching with authority! This is not like anything we have heard in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the power of the chief demon." There were no other workers.
Jesus walked through all the towns and villages. He taught in their synagogues. He preached the good news of the kingdom. And he healed all the diseases and sicknesses.
When he saw the crowds, he was deeply concerned for them. They were worn out and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is abundant, but the laborers are few.
"Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out harvesters into his harvest."
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first two are Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then comes James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
The next are Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following orders: "Do not go to the Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any Samaritan town."
Instead, go to the people of Israel. They are like sheep who have gone astray.
As you go, preach this message, "The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
He healed the sick, raised the dead, and cleansed those with leprosy, and cast out demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not take with you in your belt gold, silver, or copper.
Do not take a bag for the trip. Do not take extra clothing, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker must be provided with what they need.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If this house welcomes you, give it your blessing with peace. If it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not receive you or listen to what you have to say. If that is the case, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that town.
What I intend to tell you is true. The Day of Judgment will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be sent as messengers to the governors and kings for my sake. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when they catch you, don't worry about what to say or how to say it. At that time, the right words to say will come to you.
It's not you who should be talking, go to your father, he's the one who should be talking for you.
Brothers will deliver brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their sons. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone hates you for me. But everyone who makes it to the end will survive.
"When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not have finished going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes."
No student surpasses his teacher. No servant surpasses his master.
It is enough for a disciple to be like his teacher, and it is enough for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, what will those who live in his house expect?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed. What is hidden will be brought to light.
What I tell you in the dark, speak of it in the light. And what is whispered in your ear, proclaim it from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who has the power to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Are two birds flying from the reeds, sold only for a penny? But not a single one of those birds will land on the ground without your father knowing.
He numbers even the hairs of your head!
Do not be afraid, you are better than many sparrows.
What about a person who says in front of people that he knows me? Even I will say in front of God who is in the sky that I know Him.
Either someone says in front of people that they don't know me? I would say in front of God who is in the sky that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make sons rise up against their fathers, daughters rise up against their mothers, and daughters-in-law rise up against their mothers-in-law.
The enemy of a man is from his own family (Micah 7:6).
Anyone who loves their father or mother more than me does not deserve me. Anyone who loves their son or daughter more than me does not deserve me.
And everyone who does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. If anyone loses his life for me, he will find it.
Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives the one who sent me.
If a person receives a prophet as a prophet, he will receive the reward of a prophet. And if a person receives a pious person as a pious person, he will receive the reward of a pious person.
Assume that someone gives even a cup of cold water to a little one following me. What I am about to tell you is true. That person will certainly be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus is teaching his 12 disciples in Capernaum. After that he taught and preached in various places in Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what the Messiah was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was coming, or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John and tell him what you hear and what you see.
The blind receive their sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me.
When the disciples of John left, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John, saying, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? Did you go out to see a reed shaken by the wind?"
If not, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. The people who wear fine clothes are in the palaces of kings.
What's wrong with you, did you go out to see? A prophet? Oh, I'll tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one of whom it is written in the Holy Book: "Behold, I send My messenger before Your face, who will prepare Your way before You." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one was born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and violent people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
He who has ears should hear.
"Okay, how can I compare the people of today? It's like some kids sitting in the market, shouting at the people. They say,"
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a song of consolation for you. But you did not grieve.
When John came, his eating and drinking were like your eating and drinking. And people said, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank like you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and sinners!" And the wisdom of the Pharisees confirmed that wisdom is justified by her deeds. Cities that do not depart from iniquity.
Jesus began to speak against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. The people of those cities did not repent of their sins. And He said:
What will be the end of you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago, they would have sat in sackcloth and ashes, and they would have worn mourning clothes.
But I tell you this: the day of judgment will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to Hades. If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you this: the judgment day will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and the intelligent and revealed them to the childlike ones.
Here you go, Dad. This is what you want.
"My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And those whom the father knows are the son and those whom the son chooses to introduce them to him."
Come to me, all of you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be my servants and learn from me. For I am gentle and lowly in heart. And you will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus walked through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to pluck the heads of grain and eat.
The Pharisees saw him. They said to Jesus, "Look! For it is not lawful to do this on the Sabbath, according to the Law. But your disciples do it!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David and his men did?
He entered the house of God, he and his men, and they ate the bread of sanctity. No one was entitled to eat it except the priests.
Have you not read the law? For it is written that the priests in the temple should perform their service on every sabbath, and yet are not guilty.
I'll tell you that something more important than the temple is located here.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
After they came out of that place, Jesus entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they said to him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them: "If one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath, would you not extend your hand and pull it out?"
Man is of more value than sheep! For this reason, the law permits us to do good on the Sabbath day.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and it was restored, like the other.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all about the schemes of the Pharisees. So he left that place. Many people followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to reveal who he was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet Isaiah, who said:
Here is my servant, whom I uphold, the one I have chosen, who delights me. I will put my Spirit on him, and he will proclaim justice to the nations. He will not grow faint or be discouraged until he establishes justice on the earth.
Mash is arguing or shouting. No one hears his voice in the streets.
What does not break a bent stick, does not extinguish a low flame, is what will set everything right.
"The peoples put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42: 1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul
Jesus brought a man who was controlled by demons. The man was blind and mute. Jesus healed him. After that, the man could speak and see.
All the people were amazed and said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts. He said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself shall not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say we cast out demons by the power of Beelzebub. So by what power do your people cast them out? Then they will be your judges.
But suppose that I cast out demons by the Spirit of God; then this means that the kingdom of God has come to you.
Did you think about this? How can you enter a strong man's house and simply take what he owns? You have to tie him up first. Then, you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. He who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
This is what I tell you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you do a good deed, its fruit will be good. If you do an evil deed, its fruit will be evil. You can know the tree by its fruit.
"O nest of poisonous vipers! How can you, being evil, say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts."
A good man speaks good words. This comes from the goodness hidden within him. And a wicked man speaks wicked words. This comes from the wickedness hidden within him.
But that's what I'm telling you. On the Day of Judgment, people will have to answer for every careless word they spoke.
"By your words you will be judged or acquitted." The miraculous sign of Jonah
Then Jesus came up to him and said, "Rabbi, your son is at home in bed, suffering greatly." Jesus said to him, "I tell you
He answered: "Evil and unbelieving people demand a sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a huge fish for three days and three nights. Something like that will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The people of Nineveh will rise on the day of judgment along with the people who are living now. And the Ninevites will prove with evidence that these people are guilty. The people of Nineveh were given rest from their guilt when Jonah preached to them. And now someone is here who is greater than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with the peoples who are now alive. And she will condemn them. She came from a very distant place to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now there is someone here who is more important than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It passes through dry areas, searching for a place to rest. But it doesn't find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." When he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been swept, cleaned, and put in order.
Then the evil spirit goes out and takes with it seven other evil spirits, and they enter and dwell there. And that man becomes more wicked than before. Such is the fate of the wicked in this time." Jesus' mother and brothers.
While Jesus was still talking to the crowds, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told me, "Your mother and siblings are sitting outside, they need to talk to you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "Behold my mother! Behold my brethren!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On the same day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on a road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun came out, it burned the plants. They dried up because they did not have roots.
Another seed fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
Other seeds fell on good ground. They gave a crop that was a hundred, sixty or thirty times more than what was sown.
He who has ears, let him hear.
The disciples came to him, and asked, "Why do you use parables when you speak to people?"
He replied, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens. This has not been given to those outside."
Everyone who has this kind of knowledge will be given more. In fact, he will have very much. If no one has this kind of knowledge, even what little he has will be taken away from him.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled, who said: "You will hear and not understand, and see and not perceive."
The hearts of this people have become callous, and their ears are deaf, and their eyes are blind. And if they look for the law, and if they see it, and understand it, and turn to the Lord, then will He heal them. (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see; and your ears because they hear.
What I am about to tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people have wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like the seed that was sown on a path.
Others received the seed that fell into the rocky places. They are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it joyfully.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they cannot endure for only a short time. They quickly turn away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
Some of them received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the word. But afterward, it is crowded out by the worries of this life and the deceitful desires for wealth. It prevents them from producing fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are those who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60, or 30 times more than what the farmer planted. "The Parable of the Weeds"
Jesus told the crowd another story. "Thus is the kingdom of heaven," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did the weeds come from?
"This is the work of an enemy," he answered. The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the landowner answered. "And as you remove the saffron, you might as well remove the wheat."
Let them grow together until harvest. Then I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them: "First gather the weeds. Tie them in bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it into my barn." The Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast
Jesus told another parable to the crowd. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took it and planted it in a field.
Here is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the whole garden. It becomes a tree. And the birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another story. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through all the dough."
Jesus told all these things to the crowd using parables. He did not speak to them without telling a parable.
The words of the prophet which he spoke are these: "I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter dark sayings of old, which men of old have handed down to us: for we have received from our fathers what
Then Jesus went out to the lake with the crowd, and he entered the house. His disciples approached him and said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are uprooted and burned in the fire. Thus it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all causes of sin and all evildoers.
They will be thrown into Hellfire. There they will wail and gnash their teeth.
There the people of God will shine forth like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear. My story: the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and out of joy went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found a pearl of great value. So he went and sold all he had. And he bought that pearl. The parable of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake, and gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
This is how the Day of Judgment will be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The wicked will be thrown into the burning oven. There the wicked will wail and gnash their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He told them: "Every religious scholar who has been taught about the Kingdom of Heaven is like a house owner. He brings out of his storeroom both new and old treasures." A prophet without honor.
Jesus was having breakfast and telling these stories. Afterwards, he left that wedding party.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began teaching people in their synagogues. They were amazed and said, "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get these miraculous powers?"
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? And isn't his mother's name Mary? And aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? Where did the man get all these things from?
They did not like him at all. But Jesus told them, "A prophet is not honored in his own country. He is not given any honor in his own house."
What He did was few miracles there because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time, reports about Jesus reached Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea.
He told his disciples, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! This is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias, the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
So he promised her by an oath to give her what she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you going to say to him?" The girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a large platter."
The king was very upset. But he thought of his promise and of his dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
They brought her his head on a large tray and placed it before her. She picked it up and went with it to her mother's house.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to withdraw to rest. That is why he went in a boat to a quiet place. When the crowd heard about it, they followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples went to him and said, "This is a remote place, and it's already late. Send the crowds away to go to the villages and buy food for themselves," because we have nothing here but five loaves and two fish. Otherwise, they may collapse from exhaustion on the way." The disciples answered him, "That's impossible! We have nothing here but five loaves and two fish. And even then," he added, "most of them have small children. There's no way we can all get enough to eat from just five loaves and two fish."
Jesus answered, "It is not necessary for them to go away. You give them something to eat."
"We only have five loaves of bread and two fish," he answered.
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He divided the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And the disciples gathered twelve baskets full of the leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was about 5000. Even the women and children ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. He commanded them to precede Him to the other side of the lake of Tiberias. Then He dismissed the multitudes.
After dismissing them, he went up alone to a mountain to pray. As evening came, he was still there alone.
The boat was far from the shore. It was tossed about by waves due to the wind blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "This is a ghost!" they exclaimed. And they screamed in fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, saying, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid!"
"Sir, are you he?" Peter asked. "If you are, command me to come to you on the water."
"Give," Jesus said. Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid and began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him, and said to him, "Your faith is too weak!" "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then those in the building all prostrated themselves before the Lord Mary. And they said to him: "You are indeed the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and reached Gennesaret.
The men living there recognized Jesus. Thus, they sent a message to all the surrounding regions. The people brought all the sick to Jesus.
They begged him to allow the sick just to touch the hem of his garment. And all who touched him were healed.
Who makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why don't your students obey what the adults teach? Your students don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "Why do you not obey God's command? You want to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift dedicated to God."
They do not need to honor their parents by gifting them. You make the word of God of no value to you, so that you may follow your teachings.
"O imposters! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:"
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They only teach human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. And He said, "Hear and understand.
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." What makes you "unclean" is what comes out of your mouth.
Then His disciples came to Him. They said, "Do You know what was going through the Pharisees' minds when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads another blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Didn't you understand it yet?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. After that, it leaves the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth, they come from the heart. They are the things that make you "dirty".
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and sexual immorality in general. Also theft, false witness, and lying about others.
Here are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The faith of a Canaanite woman."
Jesus withdrew from Galilee and came to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! For my daughter is severely possessed by a demon."
Mash said Jesus a word. Thus his disciples came to him. They begged him, "Afterwards, she continues to cry over us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell in front of him on her knees. "My lord! Help me!" she said.
He replied, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to their dogs."
"Yes, my Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! As you desire, it shall be done for you." And her daughter was healed at that hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus went out from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then He went up on a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought with them blind people and those who could not walk. They even brought crippled people and those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak spoke. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk walked. The blind saw. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come together with him. And he said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have been with me for three days now. They have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will become too weak on their way back home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here." They said, "And from where will we get bread sufficient to satisfy this great multitude?"
"How many loaves do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
The seven loaves and the fish He took and blessed, then He broke them and gave them to His disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 4000. The women and children also ate a lot.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat. And he passed to the region of Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied, "In the evening, you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather is going to be nice. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and cloudy." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
An evil and untrustworthy people seeking a miraculous sign. And no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. Forgetting to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this among themselves and said, "He must be referring to this, because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying, so he said to them, "Your faith is weak! Why are you discussing among yourselves that there is no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of harvest you gathered?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets you filled?
How can you not understand? I didn't talk to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
At that time, the disciples understood that Jesus was not referring to the wine used in the bread, but rather was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus walked to Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "What are people saying about the Son of Man?"
They replied, "Some people say John the Baptist. Others say Elijah. And others say Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what business is it of yours?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered and said, "Blessed are you, Simon Barjona, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven has revealed this to you."
This is what I tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my Church. The gates of Hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them, "I must go to Jerusalem. There I will be arrested and handed over to the leading priests and teachers of religious law. I will be condemned and killed, but on the third day, I will rise from the dead."
Peter took Jesus aside, privately rebuking him and saying, "God forbid, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are setting your mind not on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come after me, they must deny themselves, take up their cross, and follow me.
If he tries to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What profit does he gain if he wins the whole world and loses his own soul? Or what does he have to exchange his soul for?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and will repay each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is real. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Changing the appearance of Jesus.
After six days, Jesus took with him Peter and James and John, the brother of James. He brought them up onto a high mountain. There they were all alone.
And there before them His appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became as white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelters: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces down.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said. "Do not be afraid."
When they raised their heads, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And while they were coming down, Jesus said to them what they should do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said to them. "Wait until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered: "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you, Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him. But everything necessary was done for him. And so will the Son of Man be treated by them.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they approached the crowd, a man stepped forward to Jesus. He knelt before him.
"Sir," he said, "have mercy on my son. He trembles violently and suffers greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought it to your children, but they couldn't find a cure for it.
"O people, you are a generation that does not believe, and your hearts are hardened!" Jesus replied. "How long shall I stay with you? How long shall I bear with you? Bring the boy to me here."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. And the boy was healed at that very hour.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. They said, "Why could we not cast out that demon?"
He replied and said, "Because your faith is very little. What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you have faith the size of a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Be removed and cast into the sea.' And it will be done. Nothing will be impossible for you."
I
They came together in Galilee. After that Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. On the third day he will rise from the dead." At this, the disciples were filled with sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, the tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Ay, he does that," he replied. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Who in authority on earth collects taxes and fees? Do they collect them from their own children or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children are not obliged to pay," Jesus told him.
But I don't like to tease them. Why do you go to the lake and throw out your net. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them on the behalf of my tax and your tax.
